[{"id":"tb-order-run","itemId":"testbench","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"The lines of an <code class=\"nobreak\">initial</code> block happen in order, each one waiting where it says. Put these in the order they happen when <code class=\"nobreak\">dff_anatomy_tb</code> runs.","explanationHtml":"<p>The stimulus block runs once, line after line (lesson 3.1). It first waits for a rising edge, then changes <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> only at falling edges, and the flip-flop copies each new <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> at the rising edge after it (lesson 2.1). The check comes after the last change, and <code class=\"nobreak\">$finish</code> comes last of all: without it, the clock would flip for ever.</p>","type":"order","steps":["The testbench waits for the first rising edge of `clk`.","At the next falling edge, `d` becomes 1.","At the next rising edge, `q` becomes 1.","After the last falling edge, the testbench checks `q` and prints its verdict.","`$finish` stops the simulation."],"shuffled":[4,2,1,0,3]},{"id":"tb-fill-period","itemId":"testbench","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">clk</code> must rise every 10 ns, like the Arty A7's clock. Type the missing delay.","explanationHtml":"<p><code class=\"nobreak\">#5</code> waits 5 ns, because the file's first line makes a time unit 1 ns, and the <code class=\"nobreak\">always</code> line flips <code class=\"nobreak\">clk</code> after each wait: 5 ns at 0, then 5 ns at 1, a 10 ns cycle (lesson 3.1). <code class=\"nobreak\">#10</code> would give a 20 ns cycle.</p>","type":"fill","file":"hdl/level-03/dff_anatomy/dff_anatomy_tb.sv","firstLine":1,"totalLines":30,"before":["`timescale 1ns/1ps","// Lesson 2.1's D flip-flop, tested as plainly as possible.","module dff_anatomy_tb;            // no ports: nothing is outside it","  logic clk, d, q;                // the signals joined to dff's ports","","  dff dut (.clk(clk), .d(d), .q(q));   // the design under test","","  initial clk = 1'b0;             // clk starts at 0..."],"blankLine":{"left":"  always ","right":" clk = ~clk;           // ...then flips, again and again"},"after":[],"highlighted":{"before":[[{"text":"`","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"timescale","color":"var(--code-token-string-expression)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"1ns","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"/","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"1ps","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// Lesson 2.1's D flip-flop, tested as plainly as possible.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"dff_anatomy_tb","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":";            ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"// no ports: nothing is outside it","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" clk, d, q;                ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"// the signals joined to dff's ports","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"dff","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" dut (.","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"clk","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":"(clk), .","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"d","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":"(d), .","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"q","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":"(q));   ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"// the design under test","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"initial","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" clk ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"1'b0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":";             ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"// clk starts at 0...","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}]],"blankLine":{"left":[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"always","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],"right":[{"text":" clk ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"~","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"clk;           ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"// ...then flips, again and again","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}]},"after":[]},"accept":["#5","# 5","#5ns","#(5)","#(5ns)"],"caseSensitive":true,"blankLabel":"The missing delay"},{"id":"tb-predict-q","itemId":"testbench","role":"core","promptHtml":"This is <code class=\"nobreak\">dff_anatomy_tb</code> running lesson 2.1's flip-flop; <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> is not drawn. The testbench sets <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> to 0, waits for the first rising edge, then sets <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> to 1, 0, 1, 1 and 0, one value at each falling edge after that. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> for each cycle.","explanationHtml":"<p>The first rising edge, at the start of cycle 0, comes before any change, so <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> takes <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code>'s first value, 0. After that, each value set at a falling edge reaches <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> at the next rising edge: 1 in cycle 1, 0 in cycle 2, then 1 and 1. The last value, 0, reaches <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> at the edge just after this window, where the testbench's one check reads it (lesson 3.1).</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"anatomy-q","source":"hdl/level-03/dff_anatomy/dff_anatomy_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"cycle","cycles":5,"signals":[{"label":"clk","path":"dff_anatomy_tb.clk","width":1,"role":"clock","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[1,"1"],[1.5,"0"],[2,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[4,"1"],[4.5,"0"]]},{"label":"q","path":"dff_anatomy_tb.q","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[2,"0"],[3,"1"]],"samples":["0","1","0","1","1"]}]},"answer":"q"},{"id":"tb-fill-wait","itemId":"testbench","role":"core","promptHtml":"This task is from the testbench of a 4-bit register, <code class=\"nobreak\">reg4_bug</code>. Each call must compare <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> with <code class=\"nobreak\">expected</code>, the value it should have, after both have changed at a rising edge (<code class=\"nobreak\">mismatch</code> is 1 when they differ). The line after each call sets the next inputs, which must be steady by the next rising edge. Type the wait that starts the task.","explanationHtml":"<p><code>@(negedge clk)</code> waits for the falling edge, half a cycle after the rising edge. By then <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">expected</code> have both changed, and inputs set there are steady long before the next rising edge (lesson 3.1). With <code>@(posedge clk)</code>, the check would come at the very moment both change, and the next inputs would change at the instant the register looks at them.</p>","type":"fill","file":"hdl/level-03/reg4_bug/reg4_bug_tb.sv","firstLine":31,"totalLines":65,"before":["  task automatic check;"],"blankLine":{"left":"    ","right":";"},"after":["    if (mismatch) begin","      $display(\"TEST FAILED: q=%b, expected %b at %0d ns (!= gave %b)\",","               q, expected, $time, loose);","      errors = errors + 1;","    end","  endtask"],"highlighted":{"before":[[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"task","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"automatic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"check","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":";","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}]],"blankLine":{"left":[{"text":"    ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],"right":[{"text":";","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}]},"after":[[{"text":"    ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"if","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" (mismatch) ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"begin","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}],[{"text":"      ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"$display","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":"(","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"\"TEST FAILED: q=","color":"var(--code-token-string-expression)","bold":false},{"text":"%b","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":", expected ","color":"var(--code-token-string-expression)","bold":false},{"text":"%b","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":" at ","color":"var(--code-token-string-expression)","bold":false},{"text":"%0d","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":" ns (!= gave ","color":"var(--code-token-string-expression)","bold":false},{"text":"%b","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":")\"","color":"var(--code-token-string-expression)","bold":false},{"text":",","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"               q, expected, ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"$time","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":", loose);","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"      errors ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" errors ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"+","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"1","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":";","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"    ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"end","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"endtask","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}]]},"accept":["@(negedge clk)","@ (negedge clk)"],"caseSensitive":true,"blankLabel":"The missing wait"},{"id":"tb-choice-stuck","itemId":"testbench","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">dff_anatomy_tb</code> sets <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> to 1, 0, 1, 1 and 0 at successive falling edges, then checks once, after the last rising edge, that <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> is 0. Which broken flip-flop would it still pass?","explanationHtml":"<p>The testbench checks one value at one moment: <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> must be 0 after the edge that takes the last <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code>, which is 0. A <code class=\"nobreak\">q</code> stuck at 0 is right at that moment, so it passes, although it never follows <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> (lesson 3.1). Stuck at 1 gives 1 there; the opposite of <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> gives 1; one edge late gives the <code class=\"nobreak\">d</code> before the last, which is also 1. Lesson 3.3's testbench, which compares after every edge, fails all four.</p>","type":"choice","options":["A flip-flop whose `q` is always 0","A flip-flop whose `q` is always 1","A flip-flop that takes the opposite of `d` at each rising edge","A flip-flop that takes `d` one rising edge late"],"correct":0}]